<h2>Questions to Ask Before Choosing a Laboratory</h2>
<p>Put the questions in writing and tie them to your sample.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Capability:</strong> Which species, sample types, and purposes are covered, and what is subcontracted?</li>
<li><strong>Validation:</strong> What is the intended use, and can I see evidence for my species and sample?</li>
<li><strong>Quality control:</strong> Which controls are used, what invalidates a batch, and how are failures investigated?</li>
<li><strong>Sample management:</strong> How are identifiers, receipt conditions, extracts, and records linked?</li>
<li><strong>Reporting:</strong> Can I see the template, validity terms, limitations, authorizer, and amendment process?</li>
<li><strong>Credentials:</strong> If accreditation is claimed, what exact scope, site, standard, and current status apply?</li>
<li><strong>Support:</strong> Who answers technical questions, and what happens after a disputed result?</li>
</ul>
<p>These are procurement questions, not accusations. SENO’s <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> is one route for sample or document questions, and the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> covers common service topics.</p>
<p>If an answer is missing, ask again rather than inferring quality from silence. A laboratory may need time to locate a document, but it should be able to say which document applies and who owns it.</p>
<h3>What We Would Do</h3>
<p>Before sending a sample, we would request the test scope, the validation summary for the named species and sample type, and the current report template. We would ask how invalid runs are handled and read the accreditation certificate and scope if accreditation is offered. If the laboratory would not describe its method, controls, or failure process at all, we would walk away. The exception is a single failed test with a documented investigation; that can show a working system rather than a broken one. A pattern of unexplained failures would change that judgment.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>Does ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation mean every test is accurate for every bird?</h3>
<p>No. Accreditation applies to a defined scope, including listed activities, sites, and methods. A test outside that scope may still be offered, but it is not automatically covered. Read the current scope for your species and sample.</p>
<h3>What is the difference between an invalid and an inconclusive test?</h3>
<p>An invalid test failed a predefined control or acceptance rule, so the result cannot be reported as a classification. An inconclusive test had a valid run but did not produce enough evidence for a confident call.</p>
<h3>How can I check whether a test was validated for my bird species?</h3>
<p>Ask for the validation or verification record for the named species group and sample type. It should state intended use, reference samples, acceptance criteria, and limitations. A general claim that a test works for birds is not species-specific evidence.</p>
<h3>Why ask for both negative and no-template controls?</h3>
<p>They can detect contamination at different stages. A negative control may carry a blank through the workflow, while a no-template control monitors reaction setup without added nucleic acid. Ask how each is prepared and what invalidates the run.</p>
<h3>Does a “detected” PCR result mean the bird is infected or diseased?</h3>
<p>Not by itself. It means the method found the declared target above its reporting rule in that sample. It does not automatically establish infection, infectiousness, or disease; clinical interpretation belongs with the attending veterinarian.</p>
<h3>Do I need chain of custody for a routine DNA sexing test?</h3>
<p>Formal custody is usually unnecessary for a companion-bird sex test, but identification still matters. The laboratory should link the sample to an identifier, record receipt and processing, and preserve the result. Formal custody matters more in disputes or regulated movement.</p>
<h3>Can a failed sample indicate a laboratory problem?</h3>
<p>It can, but it is not proof. Failure may arise from collection, storage, transport, extraction, inhibition, or a control issue. What counts is whether the laboratory investigates, gives a reason category, and takes defined corrective action.</p>
<h3>Does a bigger laboratory provide better quality?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. Scale can support automation, but it can also spread a process weakness across more samples. Evaluate scope, validation, controls, traceability, review, reporting, and failure handling at any size.</p>
<h3>Can one laboratory use another laboratory’s validation evidence?</h3>
<p>It may start with a manufacturer’s package or published evidence, but the receiving laboratory should verify performance under its own conditions. Species, sample type, reagents, equipment, and operators can all differ.</p>
<h3>What should happen if a laboratory corrects my report?</h3>
<p>A correction should produce a traceable amendment explaining what changed, when, and why. The original should remain retrievable and linked to the same accession and authorization record; a replacement PDF without history is weaker evidence.</p>
<h3>How should I compare two laboratory quotations?</h3>
<p>Compare the same intended use, species, sample type, controls, report format, repeat policy, and turnaround definition. A lower price does not compensate for a missing method description or an unclear invalid-run rule.</p>

